            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The harlot primarily symbolizes spiritual unfaithfulness, idolatry, and covenant apostasy. In the context of Revelation, the 'great harlot' is most commonly identified with imperial Rome, an apostate church, or a corrupt worldly system, though unfaithful first-century Jerusalem is a recognized minority interpretation.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The harlot symbolizes a covenant-breaker who maintains the outward form of a covenant without its substance, specifically identifying the 'great city' of Revelation as unfaithful Jerusalem.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Spiritual unfaithfulness and covenant apostasy.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A covenant-breaker, specifically unfaithful Jerusalem.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the apocalyptic harlot strictly to an entity that previously held a covenant with God (Jerusalem), excluding non-covenantal pagan empires like Rome or generic worldly systems that are often included in the consensus.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book rejects the dominant identification of the Revelation harlot as Rome or a generic world-system, insisting exclusively on Jerusalem.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book preserves the primary consensus meaning of covenant unfaithfulness but narrows its apocalyptic application exclusively to Jerusalem. By doing so, it elevates a recognized minority view (preterist Jerusalem) while explicitly rejecting the more dominant views (Rome or global systems).\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Scholars might argue that while Jerusalem perfectly fits the theological motif of covenant-breaking, the extensive commercial, geographic (seven hills), and imperial descriptions in Revelation 17–18 are more naturally suited to Rome or a broader worldly empire.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
